1
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:05,790
What are you working on?

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:07,600
Can't talk. In the zone.

3
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:09,420
Do you know what he's doing?

4
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:10,380
Could be anything.

5
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:11,410
Last time he was like this,

6
00:00:11,410 --> 00:00:13,910
he figured out electron transport in graphene.

7
00:00:13,910 --> 00:00:15,280
The time before that, he was making a list

8
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:18,140
of who's allowed in his tree fort if he ever gets one.

9
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:21,590
Still can't believe I didn't make the cut.

10
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:24,990
Uh, Sheldon, you want to take a break?

11
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:25,720
Your food's ready.

12
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:26,640
No, no, what are you doing?

13
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,940
He's both happy and quiet.

14
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:32,340
It's like seeing a unicorn and Bigfoot at the same time.

15
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:35,300
Sorry we're so late.

16
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:37,100
Bernadette got stuck at work.

17
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:37,940
Great news!

18
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:40,410
A raccoon virus just crossed the species barrier

19
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:42,930
and now can infect humans!

20
00:00:44,230 --> 00:00:45,520
Why is that great news?

21
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:47,520
In the pharmaceutical business, we have a saying:

22
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:49,600
"Mo' infections, mo' money."

23
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:51,900
Wait!

24
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:53,040
Maybe you want to wash

25
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:54,770
your little raccoon hands first.

26
00:00:54,770 --> 00:00:56,250
Oh, calm down.

27
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:58,420
If I had it, I'd be dead already.

28
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:01,570
Guys, listen up.

29
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:03,520
Can't listen, zone.

30
00:01:03,990 --> 00:01:05,860
Also don't care.

31
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:08,960
Next week is the anniversary of my first date with Bernadette.

32
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:10,720
Really don't care.

33
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:12,730
I want to do something special,

34
00:01:12,730 --> 00:01:14,500
and I was hoping you guys could be a part of it.

35
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,230
Aw, what horrible thing are you trying to make up for?

36
00:01:17,970 --> 00:01:19,400
Just putting something in the bank

37
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,970
for what horrible thing I do next.

38
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:25,110
Look, she's gonna be back any second, so here's the deal:

39
00:01:25,110 --> 00:01:26,170
I'm writing a song, and I was hoping

40
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:28,060
we could all play it for her together.

41
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:29,400
Oh, I love that.

42
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:30,250
That is so beautiful.

43
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:32,440
Sheldon?

44
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:35,580
When did we get to the Cheesecake Factory?

45
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:04,530
It's really sweet what Howard wants to do for Bernadette.

46
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:05,370
Yeah.

47
00:02:05,370 --> 00:02:06,990
Hey, how come you've never done anything romantic

48
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:08,880
to celebrate our first date?

49
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:11,940
Well, for starters, you've broken up with me so many times,

50
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:14,290
which first date are we talking about?

51
00:02:15,190 --> 00:02:18,070
Ooh, somebody call the burn ward.

52
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:21,780
And back to the zone.

53
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:25,860
And besides, I do romantic things for you all the time.

54
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,260
Can you even name one romantic thing you've done for me?

55
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:29,190
I can name tons.

56
00:02:29,190 --> 00:02:30,580
Sex doesn't count.

57
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:34,130
I know, what about that Bed-and-Breakfast?

58
00:02:34,130 --> 00:02:35,410
Well, I took you there.

59
00:02:35,410 --> 00:02:36,120
All you did was...

60
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,070
I know what I did.

61
00:02:39,010 --> 00:02:41,930
I bet they had to throw out that rocking chair.

62
00:02:43,870 --> 00:02:45,770
You know, I can be romantic if I want to.

63
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:47,230
It's fine.

64
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:49,120
And also not true.

65
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:51,830
Okay, just you wait and see.

66
00:02:51,830 --> 00:02:54,470
I'm gonna romance your freakin' ass off.

67
00:02:55,890 --> 00:02:56,520
That's beautiful.

68
00:02:56,520 --> 00:02:58,360
Is that Shakespeare?

69
00:02:58,940 --> 00:02:59,800
Sheldon?

70
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:01,480
Zone.

71
00:03:02,870 --> 00:03:05,290
He'll figure it out when he falls off the roof.

72
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:10,050
Sheldon, I know you're in the zone,

73
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:11,840
but do you want some tea?

74
00:03:15,910 --> 00:03:17,160
All right, I can't believe I'm gonna say this,

75
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:19,850
but you haven't spoken in hours

76
00:03:19,850 --> 00:03:20,780
and I'm starting to get worried.

77
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:22,060
Please say something.

78
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:23,720
Leonard, prepare to be humbled

79
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:26,710
and weep at the glory of my genius.

80
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:30,640
Nope, it was better before.

81
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:32,870
Look at it.

82
00:03:33,230 --> 00:03:36,030
I feel like my mind just made a baby.

83
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:39,350
And it's beautiful.

84
00:03:39,350 --> 00:03:40,550
It's not like human babies,

85
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:43,540
which are loud and covered in goop.

86
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:45,560
Holy crap, Sheldon,

87
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:47,210
did you just figure out a method

88
00:03:47,210 --> 00:03:50,190
for synthesizing a new stable super-heavy element?

89
00:03:50,790 --> 00:03:52,370
Did I?

90
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:54,000
Well, that can't be right.

91
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,420
No one's ever done that before.

92
00:03:55,420 --> 00:03:57,290
Except me, because I just did it!

93
00:03:57,290 --> 00:03:59,520
Sheldon and his brain-- yeah!

94
00:03:59,530 --> 00:04:01,240
Sheldon and his brain-- yeah!

95
00:04:01,250 --> 00:04:02,880
Sheldon and his brain-- yeah!

96
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:04,400
Sheldon and his brain-- yeah!

97
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:05,240
Sheldon and his brain...

98
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:07,000
Yeah, definitely better before.

99
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:11,610
So I'm almost done writing the song for Bernadette.

100
00:04:11,610 --> 00:04:12,960
Are you cool playing the cello?

101
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:15,750
If by "cool" you mean "willing to," Yes.

102
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:17,210
If by "cool" You mean "cool,"

103
00:04:17,210 --> 00:04:19,350
clearly you've never seen me play the cello.

104
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:22,210
Great. Will you play the ukulele?

105
00:04:22,210 --> 00:04:25,460
Of course. I'd be happy to shred it on my ax.

106
00:04:26,830 --> 00:04:29,810
Or you could just play your tiny, ridiculous guitar.

107
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:35,070
Fine. I will melt her heart.

108
00:04:35,070 --> 00:04:37,180
And her face!

109
00:04:39,330 --> 00:04:40,880
Gentlemen.

110
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:43,280
Uh, no doubt you heard about my little breakthrough.

111
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:44,600
Now, if your plan

112
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,440
is to hoist me on your shoulders

113
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:49,100
and carry me around the cafeteria, please refrain.

114
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:51,350
I don't care for heights, motion sickness

115
00:04:51,350 --> 00:04:54,200
or the thought of your necks touching my buttocks.

116
00:04:55,870 --> 00:04:57,580
Seriously, congratulations, Sheldon.

117
00:04:57,580 --> 00:04:59,040
Yeah, I read your paper online.

118
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:01,950
That technique for creating a new heavy element is inspired.

119
00:05:01,950 --> 00:05:02,850
Oh, well, thank you.

120
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:03,770
Believe it or not,

121
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:06,260
I just learned a Chinese research team

122
00:05:06,260 --> 00:05:08,000
at the Hubei Institute for Nuclear Physics

123
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:09,880
ran a test on a cyclotron,

124
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:11,910
and the results were extremely promising.

125
00:05:11,910 --> 00:05:14,260
- Sheldon, that's incredible. - Yeah, I know!

126
00:05:14,260 --> 00:05:17,520
They called it the greatest thing since the Communist party.

127
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:19,780
Although I'm pretty sure that

128
00:05:19,780 --> 00:05:21,750
the Communist party made them say that.

129
00:05:23,130 --> 00:05:24,440
I like China.

130
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:27,180
See, they know how to keep people in line.

131
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:29,760
So, what happens next?

132
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:32,720
Oh, more testing, more success, more fame.

133
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:33,600
But don't worry,

134
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:36,720
I will remain the same down-to-earth humble Joe

135
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:38,570
I've always been.

136
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:40,330
- Good to know. - Yeah.

137
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:41,030
Now give me that cookie;

138
00:05:41,030 --> 00:05:42,330
I discovered an element.

139
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,990
Thank you so much for coming.

140
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:49,010
You called the right person.

141
00:05:49,010 --> 00:05:50,780
I believe I have the perfect romantic evening

142
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:51,880
for you to give Leonard.

143
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:54,220
Okay, good, 'cause I've been really struggling with this.

144
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:55,480
As I'm sure you're aware,

145
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:58,370
the quickest way to a man's heart is through his...

146
00:05:58,370 --> 00:06:01,060
Pants, but Leonard says sex doesn't count.

147
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:04,030
You poor thing.

148
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:05,210
You have one arrow in your quiver,

149
00:06:05,210 --> 00:06:06,960
and you just can't use it.

150
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:10,730
Fortunately, another pathway to a man's heart

151
00:06:10,730 --> 00:06:12,980
is through his stomach.

152
00:06:13,410 --> 00:06:14,570
I don't know if I want to cook for him;

153
00:06:14,570 --> 00:06:15,970
he's kind of a picky eater.

154
00:06:15,970 --> 00:06:17,460
I mean, "It's too salty.

155
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:18,630
It's too dry.

156
00:06:18,630 --> 00:06:22,090
It's too burnt and frozen at the same time."

157
00:06:23,130 --> 00:06:23,620
Wait, come on,

158
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:25,210
what else would sweep you off your feet?

159
00:06:25,210 --> 00:06:28,930
Well, I've always had this fantasy that involves dancing.

160
00:06:28,930 --> 00:06:31,170
The sexual chemistry

161
00:06:31,170 --> 00:06:34,260
between my partner and me is electric.

162
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:35,280
But boy, oh, boy,

163
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:36,340
does my father not approve

164
00:06:36,340 --> 00:06:39,220
until he sees us in the big dance competition.

165
00:06:40,530 --> 00:06:43,170
Okay, that's just the plot for Dirty Dancing.

166
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:45,310
What else would you love?

167
00:06:45,310 --> 00:06:48,290
Other than being lifted over Patrick Swayze's head.

168
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:52,010
Oh! Oh, uh, you could, uh, stand outside his window

169
00:06:52,010 --> 00:06:54,720
with a boom box in the air.

170
00:06:56,900 --> 00:06:59,200
That's from Say Anything.

171
00:06:59,810 --> 00:07:02,040
Look, I'm a lonely guy; I watch a lot of movies.

172
00:07:03,710 --> 00:07:06,660
Look, Penny, if you truly want to be romantic,

173
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:08,110
it needs to come from you.

174
00:07:08,110 --> 00:07:10,680
I get that, but why is this so hard?

175
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:12,720
Well, you've probably never had to do this stuff

176
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:13,660
'cause you're young and beautiful

177
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:16,200
and men have always thrown themselves at you.

178
00:07:17,810 --> 00:07:19,180
Yeah, I'm trying to be sad about that.

179
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:20,770
I can't.

180
00:07:25,450 --> 00:07:27,190
Let's see, what's next?

181
00:07:27,190 --> 00:07:28,240
Okay! Here.

182
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:30,020
This is the Magic Marker

183
00:07:30,020 --> 00:07:32,290
I was using when I made the discovery.

184
00:07:34,250 --> 00:07:36,730
I don't think the Smithsonian's gonna want your marker.

185
00:07:36,730 --> 00:07:39,640
And that's why you're not on a list for my tree fort.

186
00:07:43,830 --> 00:07:45,820
Ooh, guess who's getting an article

187
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:47,870
written about him in Physics Today?

188
00:07:47,870 --> 00:07:49,100
I'll give you a hint.

189
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:52,700
You measured the diameter of his suspicious mole yesterday.

190
00:07:54,620 --> 00:07:56,140
Sheldon, I'm so proud of you.

191
00:07:56,140 --> 00:07:57,040
Well, you should be.

192
00:07:57,040 --> 00:07:59,150
My discovery is spreading like wildfire.

193
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:01,370
Unlike my mole, which is holding steady

194
00:08:01,370 --> 00:08:03,270
at the size of a navy bean.

195
00:08:04,670 --> 00:08:05,700
What's next?

196
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:07,870
This is the very copy

197
00:08:07,870 --> 00:08:10,050
of The Handbook of Chemistry and Physics

198
00:08:10,050 --> 00:08:11,790
in which I looked up the reaction rates

199
00:08:11,790 --> 00:08:13,890
of mendelevium and...

200
00:08:15,550 --> 00:08:17,860
- And what? - No.

201
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:20,140
No, no, no, no.

202
00:08:20,690 --> 00:08:22,220
What's wrong?

203
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:25,440
I've made a horrible mistake.

204
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:26,780
What are you talking about?

205
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:29,550
This table-- it's in square centimeters.

206
00:08:29,550 --> 00:08:31,310
I read it as square meters.

207
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:32,820
You know what that means?

208
00:08:32,820 --> 00:08:35,820
That Americans can't handle the metric system?

209
00:08:37,650 --> 00:08:41,150
Amy, I was off by a factor of 10,000.

210
00:08:41,150 --> 00:08:43,530
But the Chinese team found the element.

211
00:08:43,530 --> 00:08:44,650
Yeah, well, they shouldn't have.

212
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:46,430
My calculations were wrong.

213
00:08:46,430 --> 00:08:47,390
There must be some resonance

214
00:08:47,390 --> 00:08:49,620
between the elements I didn't know about.

215
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:51,210
So you just got lucky?

216
00:08:51,210 --> 00:08:54,350
Sheldon Cooper does not get lucky!

217
00:08:55,730 --> 00:08:57,800
You and me both, brother.

218
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:00,060
It doesn't matter.

219
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:01,680
The element was found because of you,

220
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:03,070
and that's groundbreaking.

221
00:09:03,070 --> 00:09:04,450
What matters is

222
00:09:04,450 --> 00:09:06,460
the greatest scientific achievement of my life

223
00:09:06,460 --> 00:09:08,090
is based on a blunder.

224
00:09:08,090 --> 00:09:11,440
I'm not a genius, I'm a fraud.

225
00:09:12,070 --> 00:09:13,580
You know, Sheldon, in neuroscience,

226
00:09:13,580 --> 00:09:15,800
we're forever finding something in one part of the brain

227
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:17,450
that we thought was someplace else.

228
00:09:17,450 --> 00:09:18,370
Oh, great.

229
00:09:18,370 --> 00:09:20,600
Now I'm worse than a fraud.

230
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:22,740
I'm practically a biologist.

231
00:09:28,610 --> 00:09:30,580
You got to stop beating yourself up over this.

232
00:09:30,580 --> 00:09:33,250
I mean, you made a mistake, but it was a happy mistake.

233
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:35,190
There's nothing happy about it.

234
00:09:35,190 --> 00:09:37,810
I'm being given credit that I don't deserve.

235
00:09:37,810 --> 00:09:40,860
Oh, people get things they don't deserve all the time.

236
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:43,320
Look at me with you.

237
00:09:43,650 --> 00:09:46,320
No, Leonard, this is going to haunt me for the rest of my life.

238
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:48,010
Every science classroom,

239
00:09:48,010 --> 00:09:50,840
every periodic table, every textbook--

240
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:53,780
this mistake will be staring back at me

241
00:09:53,780 --> 00:09:57,550
like that time you let Koothrappali wax your eyebrows.

242
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:01,820
I've got to find a way to stop this thing.

243
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:03,270
Buddy, I don't think you can.

244
00:10:03,270 --> 00:10:05,660
I mean, once it's out there, it's out there.

245
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:09,260
This thing is like the science equivalent of a sex tape.

246
00:10:10,210 --> 00:10:12,300
You know, frankly, I'd prefer a sex tape.

247
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:14,000
You don't know what a sex tape is, do you?

248
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,160
No.

249
00:10:18,050 --> 00:10:19,550
Make sure you guys get to the restaurant by 8:00.

250
00:10:19,550 --> 00:10:21,120
Bernadette's meeting me there at 8:30

251
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:22,420
and I don't want to blow the surprise.

252
00:10:22,420 --> 00:10:23,860
- We'll be there. - Got it.

253
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:26,710
Now, uh, I have a question about the song.

254
00:10:26,710 --> 00:10:28,090
I was thinking at some point

255
00:10:28,090 --> 00:10:31,450
I could bust out a little rap.

256
00:10:32,170 --> 00:10:34,990
N-N-N-No...

257
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:40,110
Come on, come on, you haven't even heard it yet.

258
00:10:40,110 --> 00:10:42,110
Leonard, give me a beat.

259
00:10:42,110 --> 00:10:44,120
I will not.

260
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:46,110
Please?

261
00:10:46,110 --> 00:10:47,830
No, I want this to be romantic.

262
00:10:47,830 --> 00:10:48,750
It will be!

263
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:50,480
I don't call anyone a ho,

264
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:52,560
and the only time I use the phrase "my bitch,"

265
00:10:52,560 --> 00:10:54,610
I'm referring to you.

266
00:10:56,070 --> 00:10:57,390
There he is!

267
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:01,350
Stop it.

268
00:11:01,350 --> 00:11:03,200
Stop celebrating me!

269
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:06,870
And no woos!

270
00:11:06,870 --> 00:11:09,350
Oh, not you, Dr. Woo. You're fine.

271
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:14,220
I want you all to know

272
00:11:14,220 --> 00:11:16,540
that you have no reason to applaud me.

273
00:11:16,540 --> 00:11:17,740
My so-called breakthrough

274
00:11:17,740 --> 00:11:19,730
wasn't the result of my genius.

275
00:11:19,730 --> 00:11:22,380
It was nothing more than a boneheaded mistake.

276
00:11:22,380 --> 00:11:24,380
So please refrain

277
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:26,300
from praising me for it in the future.

278
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:27,230
Wh... I don't understand.

279
00:11:27,230 --> 00:11:28,630
They didn't find the element?

280
00:11:28,630 --> 00:11:30,930
Oh, no, they found the element.

281
00:11:31,150 --> 00:11:33,060
No, no, stop it!

282
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:34,430
I don't need to take this admiration

283
00:11:34,430 --> 00:11:36,230
from the likes of you people!

284
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:39,300
How do I make them stop loving me?

285
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:41,600
Invite them to live with us.

286
00:11:44,950 --> 00:11:47,710
- Boy, Bernadette is gonna love this. - Yeah.

287
00:11:47,710 --> 00:11:51,100
It must be nice to have someone do something so romantic.

288
00:11:51,870 --> 00:11:53,480
Okay, you know what's not romantic?

289
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:55,150
Rubbing it in someone's face.

290
00:11:55,150 --> 00:11:56,170
Actually, it can be,

291
00:11:56,170 --> 00:11:58,920
but I told you sex doesn't count.

292
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:02,600
I'm getting worried about Bernadette.

293
00:12:02,600 --> 00:12:04,970
I'm gonna call and check on her.

294
00:12:05,490 --> 00:12:06,980
The National Science Foundation

295
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:08,970
wants to give me a substantial grant.

296
00:12:08,970 --> 00:12:11,190
- Oh, that's a big deal. - I know.

297
00:12:11,190 --> 00:12:13,420
When will this nightmare end?

298
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:16,330
Hey, I get that you feel bad about all the attention,

299
00:12:16,330 --> 00:12:18,240
but still, what you did is amazing.

300
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:20,990
- We're really proud of you. - I'm not.

301
00:12:22,120 --> 00:12:23,260
You're not?

302
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:25,480
Sheldon, I've been thinking about it,

303
00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:26,950
and you're right.

304
00:12:26,950 --> 00:12:28,930
You don't deserve any credit.

305
00:12:28,930 --> 00:12:31,740
All you did was misread some numbers on a table.

306
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:33,710
A very easy table, too.

307
00:12:33,710 --> 00:12:37,640
Honestly, I'm embarrassed for you.

308
00:12:38,710 --> 00:12:42,080
That's the most romantic thing I've ever heard.

309
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:46,010
Damn it, everyone's better at this than me.

310
00:12:46,730 --> 00:12:48,370
Congratulations, Dr. Fowler.

311
00:12:48,370 --> 00:12:50,400
You just made the fort.

312
00:12:53,310 --> 00:12:55,760
Guys, there w-was an accident at Bernie's lab.

313
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:56,960
Oh, my God, is she okay?

314
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:59,490
Yeah, but she's at the hospital in quarantine.

315
00:12:59,490 --> 00:13:01,300
- What? - Poor Bernadette.

316
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:05,440
I hate to pile on the bad news, but I just got a raise.

317
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:16,730
Oh, my God, Bernie, what happened?

318
00:13:16,730 --> 00:13:18,140
Well, let's just say the next time you move

319
00:13:18,140 --> 00:13:20,360
a dozen vials of raccoon virus to the fridge,

320
00:13:20,360 --> 00:13:22,290
make two trips.

321
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:25,490
You're sure you're okay?

322
00:13:25,490 --> 00:13:26,960
Yeah, it's just a precaution.

323
00:13:26,960 --> 00:13:27,640
If there was a problem,

324
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:30,600
I'd be throwing up out of my eyeballs by now.

325
00:13:32,050 --> 00:13:33,650
Sorry I messed up our date.

326
00:13:33,650 --> 00:13:35,120
No, don't worry about it.

327
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:37,020
I brought the date here.

328
00:13:37,020 --> 00:13:38,780
Guys, come on in.

329
00:13:39,700 --> 00:13:40,830
What's all this?

330
00:13:40,830 --> 00:13:44,100
Well, tonight is the anniversary of our first date,

331
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:48,170
and I wanted to celebrate it by writing a song for you.

332
00:13:48,170 --> 00:13:49,540
Howie.

333
00:13:49,540 --> 00:13:51,650
Sheldon, get over here!

334
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,220
She might be contagious.

335
00:13:55,190 --> 00:13:57,610
Don't you think I'm having a rough enough day?

336
00:13:57,610 --> 00:13:59,400
Sheldon!

337
00:14:10,230 --> 00:14:12,270
* If I didn't have you *

338
00:14:12,270 --> 00:14:13,930
* Life would be blue *

339
00:14:13,930 --> 00:14:17,240
* I'd be Dr. Who without the TARDIS *

340
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:19,510
Is it me, or does she not look so good?

341
00:14:20,450 --> 00:14:21,840
* A candle without a wick *

342
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:23,400
* A Watson without a Crick *

343
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:26,600
* I'd be one of my outfits without a dick-ie *

344
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:28,290
* I'd be cheese without the mac *

345
00:14:28,290 --> 00:14:30,930
* Jobs without the Wozniak *

346
00:14:30,930 --> 00:14:32,250
* I'd be solving exponential equations *

347
00:14:32,250 --> 00:14:34,010
* That use bases not found on your calculator *

348
00:14:34,010 --> 00:14:36,840
* Making it much harder to crack *

349
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:38,840
* I'd be an atom without a bomb  *

350
00:14:38,840 --> 00:14:40,440
* A dot without the com *

351
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:43,720
* And I'd probably still live with my mom *

352
00:14:43,720 --> 00:14:46,650
* And he'd probably still live with his mom *

353
00:14:47,550 --> 00:14:50,580
* Ever since I met you, you turned my world around *

354
00:14:50,580 --> 00:14:53,770
* You supported all my dreams and all my hopes *

355
00:14:53,770 --> 00:14:57,100
* You're like uranium-235 and I'm uranium-238 *

356
00:14:57,100 --> 00:15:02,470
* Almost inseparable isotopes *

357
00:15:04,090 --> 00:15:06,980
* I couldn't have imagined *

358
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:10,180
* How good my life would get *

359
00:15:10,180 --> 00:15:11,760
* From the moment *

360
00:15:11,760 --> 00:15:13,690
* That I met you *

361
00:15:13,690 --> 00:15:15,100
* Bernadette *

362
00:15:15,100 --> 00:15:16,120
Oh, Howie.

363
00:15:16,120 --> 00:15:18,300
* If I didn't have you *

364
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:20,010
* Life would be dreary *

365
00:15:20,010 --> 00:15:23,000
* I'd be string theory without any string *

366
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:24,510
* I'd be binary code without a one *

367
00:15:24,510 --> 00:15:26,260
* A cathode ray tube without an electron gun *

368
00:15:26,260 --> 00:15:29,270
* I'd be Firefly, Buffy and Avengers without Joss Whedon *

369
00:15:29,270 --> 00:15:31,010
* I'd speak a lot more Klingon *

370
00:15:31,010 --> 00:15:34,530
* Heghlu'meH QaQ jajvam *

371
00:15:34,530 --> 00:15:38,690
* And he'd definitely still live with his mom *

372
00:15:38,690 --> 00:15:41,960
* Ever since I met you, you turned my world around *

373
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:44,920
* You're my best friend and my lover *

374
00:15:44,920 --> 00:15:48,910
* We're like changing electric and magnetic fields *

375
00:15:48,910 --> 00:15:51,190
* You can't have one *

376
00:15:51,830 --> 00:15:56,470
* Without the other *

377
00:15:56,850 --> 00:15:59,550
* I couldn't have imagined *

378
00:15:59,550 --> 00:16:02,710
* How good my life would get *

379
00:16:02,710 --> 00:16:06,110
* From the moment that I met you *

380
00:16:06,110 --> 00:16:07,800
* Bernadette *

381
00:16:09,230 --> 00:16:12,910
* Oh, we couldn't have imagined *

382
00:16:12,910 --> 00:16:16,180
* How good our lives would get *

383
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:19,640
* From the moment that we met you *

384
00:16:19,640 --> 00:16:23,240
* Bernadette. *

385
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:31,890
Howie, that was amazing!

386
00:16:31,890 --> 00:16:33,510
Look, I'm shaking.

387
00:16:33,510 --> 00:16:35,800
She's sick, I knew it. Bye!

388
00:16:40,510 --> 00:16:41,360
I have to say,

389
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:43,630
this is the best top Ramen you've ever made.

390
00:16:45,150 --> 00:16:46,830
I discovered a secret ingredient:

391
00:16:46,830 --> 00:16:48,720
the flavor packet.

392
00:16:49,930 --> 00:16:51,910
That sucker is well named.

393
00:16:54,610 --> 00:16:55,880
All right, lover boy.

394
00:16:55,880 --> 00:16:58,060
Get ready, 'cause there is a crap storm of romance

395
00:16:58,060 --> 00:16:59,520
coming your way.

396
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:02,780
Stop it, you're gonna make me cry.

397
00:17:05,130 --> 00:17:07,340
All right. You seem pretty confident.

398
00:17:07,340 --> 00:17:08,940
Oh, I am.

399
00:17:09,330 --> 00:17:12,410
Maybe if you follow this trail,

400
00:17:12,410 --> 00:17:14,200
you'll see why.

401
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:17,030
- Oh, rose petals. - Yes.

402
00:17:17,030 --> 00:17:17,880
The most beautiful

403
00:17:17,880 --> 00:17:20,800
and shockingly expensive of all the flowers.

404
00:17:23,290 --> 00:17:24,260
You made the bed?

405
00:17:24,260 --> 00:17:26,620
You really are pulling out all the stops.

406
00:17:27,710 --> 00:17:30,180
Okay, I gave this a lot of thought,

407
00:17:30,180 --> 00:17:32,580
and I finally found something to show you

408
00:17:32,580 --> 00:17:34,450
how much I love you.

409
00:17:38,670 --> 00:17:42,100
It's a first edition of

410
00:17:42,100 --> 00:17:44,160
Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy.

411
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:47,220
I remember it was your favorite book growing up.

412
00:17:47,870 --> 00:17:49,240
This is great.

413
00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:50,470
It... thank you.

414
00:17:50,470 --> 00:17:52,620
It's... so much.

415
00:17:52,620 --> 00:17:54,910
What?

416
00:17:54,910 --> 00:17:56,770
- Nothing. I love it. - No.

417
00:17:56,770 --> 00:17:58,540
Something's going on. What's wrong with it?

418
00:17:58,540 --> 00:18:00,040
I remember you saying how great it would be

419
00:18:00,040 --> 00:18:01,600
to have a first edition.

420
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:03,090
It's true, I-I did.

421
00:18:03,090 --> 00:18:04,720
I did say that...

422
00:18:04,720 --> 00:18:07,210
when we were at the used bookstore together

423
00:18:07,210 --> 00:18:10,200
and I saw the first edition and I bought it.

424
00:18:11,850 --> 00:18:15,530
- Oh, my God, I am the worst! - No, no, it's okay.

425
00:18:15,530 --> 00:18:16,530
It's really thoughtful.

426
00:18:16,530 --> 00:18:18,210
No, it's not! I mean...

427
00:18:19,650 --> 00:18:22,820
What's thoughtful is everything you do.

428
00:18:24,090 --> 00:18:26,350
Here, you know what? Look at this.

429
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:30,270
Look, here's the... the plane ticket you bought me

430
00:18:30,270 --> 00:18:32,630
when I was too poor to go home for the holidays.

431
00:18:33,210 --> 00:18:35,550
And the rose you left on my windshield...

432
00:18:35,550 --> 00:18:37,390
just because.

433
00:18:37,390 --> 00:18:39,740
Here's the-the thank-you letter you wrote me

434
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:41,700
after the first time I slept with you.

435
00:18:43,100 --> 00:18:45,300
All 11 pages of it.

436
00:18:48,130 --> 00:18:50,640
Can't believe you saved all this stuff.

437
00:18:51,060 --> 00:18:52,300
Of course I did.

438
00:18:52,300 --> 00:18:53,940
It's you.

439
00:18:55,650 --> 00:18:56,880
Come here.

440
00:19:02,010 --> 00:19:04,460
Is that a pregnancy test?

441
00:19:04,460 --> 00:19:05,410
Oh, yeah, just the first one.

442
00:19:05,410 --> 00:19:07,300
I didn't save them all.

443
00:19:12,830 --> 00:19:15,930
I can't believe I read this table wrong.

444
00:19:15,930 --> 00:19:17,720
I blame you.

445
00:19:19,450 --> 00:19:21,130
Me? What did I do?

446
00:19:21,130 --> 00:19:22,620
You distract me.

447
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:25,530
I've been distracted since the moment I met you.

448
00:19:27,150 --> 00:19:28,020
Sorry.

449
00:19:28,020 --> 00:19:29,530
Well, you should be.

450
00:19:29,530 --> 00:19:30,710
Because all I can think about

451
00:19:30,710 --> 00:19:33,090
is how much I want to kiss you.

452
00:19:36,160 --> 00:19:38,640
And not just on the cheek, but on the mouth.

453
00:19:42,420 --> 00:19:44,940
Like mommies and daddies do.

454
00:19:48,060 --> 00:19:50,220
Oh, Sheldon.

455
00:19:54,860 --> 00:19:55,910
Amy?

456
00:19:58,820 --> 00:19:59,620
Amy.

457
00:20:00,710 --> 00:20:02,220
Did you hear what I said?

458
00:20:02,220 --> 00:20:04,180
Can't talk, in the zone.

